WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.203
Hi, I'm Dewdrop!

00:00:02.203 --> 00:00:06.464
This language model starts from blanks and fills them in, not left to right.

00:00:09.683 --> 00:00:13.610
Train one Transformer to fill in any fraction of blanks, then write

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.246
by unmasking the confident ones.

00:00:18.858 --> 00:00:20.272
Training is fill in the blanks.

00:00:20.858 --> 00:00:25.014
Mask each token with probability t, and predict every original at once.

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:29.017
t covers every ratio, and dividing by t makes the loss

00:00:29.017 --> 00:00:30.320
a bound on likelihood.

00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:35.616
LLaDA's eight billion parameters have exactly the shapes of a left-to-right model.

00:00:35.616 --> 00:00:38.604
That's why Dream could start from an existing one.

00:00:40.425 --> 00:00:43.653
Append masks to the prompt, and run the whole sequence through once.

00:00:44.073 --> 00:00:47.955
Every masked slot gets a prediction and a confidence, reading both sides.

00:00:48.375 --> 00:00:49.853
Commit the most confident.

00:00:49.853 --> 00:00:51.331
Those are never revisited.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.466
Remask the rest, and run again.

00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:02.147
A left-to-right model reads only the past, which never changes, so it

00:01:02.147 --> 00:01:03.502
caches keys and values.

00:01:03.922 --> 00:01:08.071
Here every token reads every other, so each commit moves every key and value.

00:01:08.071 --> 00:01:09.849
Each step is a full pass.

00:01:11.358 --> 00:01:14.615
Diffusing the whole answer means full passes and a fixed length.

00:01:15.865 --> 00:01:18.722
Block diffusion runs left to right over blocks.

00:01:18.722 --> 00:01:21.578
Finished blocks never change, so they cache exactly.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:27.586
Speed comes from committing several tokens per pass: about three with a

00:01:27.586 --> 00:01:29.854
confidence threshold, nearly six with self-checking.

00:01:31.142 --> 00:01:34.341
A Transformer without its causal mask, trained to fill any blanks.

00:01:34.341 --> 00:01:37.249
Its speed is how many it can commit per pass.

00:01:40.275 --> 00:01:44.684
A weighted fill-in loss, confident unmasking, and blocks that bring the cache back.

00:01:45.104 --> 00:01:47.205
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:47.205 --> 00:01:47.805
I'm Dewdrop.

00:01:47.805 --> 00:01:48.105
Bye!
